2. Support posture and production maturity (must-have)
How mature, stable, and production-proven is the tool and its Confluent-related support model? Is support official, community-based, bridged through another provider, or dependent on custom scripting?

Why it matters: Your story explicitly prioritizes production maturity and calls out verification of provider/support posture.

3. State management and drift control (must-have)
How well does the approach maintain a known desired state, detect drift, reconcile changes, and avoid configuration divergence between declared configuration and actual Confluent Cloud resources?

Why it matters: This is the IaC equivalent of your earlier drift criterion and is critical for production governance.

4. Operational simplicity (must-have)
How many tools, abstractions, runtimes, wrappers, and operating concepts must engineers manage day to day? How easy is it to onboard, troubleshoot, and run in CI/CD?

Why it matters: Your assumptions explicitly prioritize operational simplicity and warn against unnecessary complexity.

5. Maintainability and code model
How easy is it to structure, understand, review, extend, and reuse the configuration over time? Does the tool encourage clean modularization, reduce duplication, and support team-scale change safely?

Why it matters: Production suitability depends as much on maintainability as on raw capability.

6. Environment and tenancy management
How clearly can the approach represent differences across environments such as DEV, TEST, and PROD, and across regions, business units, or Confluent environments/accounts?

Why it matters: This replaces the Kubernetes-oriented environment criterion with one better suited to IaC operating models.

7. Dependency handling and orchestration
How well can the tool model resource dependencies, creation order, readiness constraints, and cross-resource references — especially where Confluent Cloud resources depend on GCP networking or identity prerequisites?

Why it matters: Ordering still matters, but now at the cloud resource/API level rather than Helm release sequencing.

8. Governance, policy, and approval workflow support
How well does the approach support peer review, plan/apply separation, policy enforcement, approval gates, and integration with enterprise CI/CD and change-management processes?

Why it matters: This is a major differentiator between ad hoc automation and production-grade IaC.

9. Auditability and change traceability
How clearly can teams see what changed, when, by whom, and through which pipeline or approval path? Does the approach naturally support version control, change review, execution logs, and traceable deployments?

Why it matters: This should remain from your previous criteria almost unchanged.

10. Secrets and credential handling
How safely and practically does the tool handle Confluent API keys, GCP credentials, service principals/service accounts, and integration with enterprise secret stores?

Why it matters: For Confluent Cloud tooling, credential sprawl and unsafe secret handling are real production risks.

11. Multi-cloud and cloud-agnostic operating model fit (nice-to-have)
How well can the same approach be used across GCP, AWS, and Azure at the operating-model level, even if resource definitions are not identical? Can teams reuse patterns, workflows, module structure, and governance across clouds?

Why it matters: Your story explicitly says this is a nice-to-have, not the primary decision driver.

12. Integration with GCP and surrounding platform tooling
How well does the option integrate with the wider delivery ecosystem, such as GCP resource provisioning, IAM, networking, CI/CD platforms, policy tooling, remote state backends, and developer workflows?

Why it matters: Confluent Cloud rarely stands alone; it usually depends on surrounding cloud infrastructure.

13. Extensibility and escape hatches
If a required Confluent capability is missing or lagging, how easily can the approach be extended using APIs, custom modules, wrappers, external scripts, or mixed models without breaking maintainability?

Why it matters: Important for evaluating Pulumi, OpenTofu, and CLI/API automation fairly.

14. Upgrade and ecosystem sustainability
How manageable is the long-term lifecycle of the tool itself — provider updates, breaking changes, ecosystem direction, licensing model, plugin maintenance, and compatibility with surrounding tooling?

Why it matters: Especially relevant for OpenTofu, Terragrunt, and any solution dependent on provider bridging.

15. Recommended usage boundaries / fit-for-purpose scope
For which use cases is the tool a strong fit, and where should it not be used? For example: primary declarative provisioning, wrapper/orchestration only, gap-filling automation, or one-off operational tasks.

Why it matters: Your acceptance criteria explicitly require decision boundaries.

